      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost per Square Foot by Material"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The material you choose has the biggest impact on your bottom line. Utah pricing averages $4.50 to $18.00 per square foot fully installed:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles ($4.50 \u2013 $8.25 / sq. ft.): The standard choice for Utah climates. Architectural shingles offer good wind and snow resistance and last 20 to 30 years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Class 4 Impact-Resistant Shingles ($6.00 \u2013 $9.00 / sq. ft.): Highly recommended if you live in hail-prone areas like parts of Davis or Weber counties. They cost roughly 15% to 25% more but can lower your home insurance premiums."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing ($8.00 \u2013 $17.00 / sq. ft.): A premium option that shines in high-altitude, high-snow areas (like Park City or Heber) because snow slides right off. They last 40 to 70 years."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Utah-Specific Price Drivers"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Snow Load & Pitch: Steeper roofs require extra safety harnesses and labor. In Utah, a steep pitch or complex roof design with many valleys can add 15% to 30% to your labor bill ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Location: Labor rates fluctuate by county. Roofs along the Salt Lake County metro area typically cost slightly more due to localized labor demands and permitting fees compared to northern regions like Cache County ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hidden Decking Damage: In older Utah homes, ice dams can rot the underlying wood (OSB/plywood). Expect to pay $60 to $80 per sheet if your contractor discovers rotted decking after tearing off the old shingles."
          }
        ]
      },
